fix(hook): record a capture that failed (#277)

The stop-time capture reported every refusal and every failure through warn(),
and warn() writes to the stderr of a SubagentStop, which the harness discards.
The hook is also forbidden to exit 2 on a stop -- nine invocations, measured --
so it could not report by refusing either. A refused capture, a failed
signature and a clean tree were therefore one thing seen from outside: nothing.
Measured, two subagents stopped within the same minute; one was captured and
committed, the other was not, and the only evidence of the second was that its
files were missing hours later. That is the I3 breach: an action was attempted
and abandoned, and nothing recorded it.

A capture attempt that does not end in a commit now appends one line to
$INNSEGL_RUNS_DIR/uncaptured.jsonl, overridable as INNSEGL_UNCAPTURED_LOG and
written 0600, naming the run, the reason, the worktree the ledger knows it by,
the tree on this machine, the dirty, written, staged and unaccounted counts,
and the paths on each side -- enough to redo or recover the work. One reason
per branch of the capture: bodies_unreadable, index_unreadable,
no_recorded_writes, index_unaccounted, staging_unaccounted, nothing_uncommitted
and signing_failed.

The signer's own output is kept rather than shouted. It went straight to that
same discarded stderr, so the one sentence saying WHY a signature failed -- a
run already retired, a deployment that is not up, a credential the listener
refused -- was the first thing lost, and a stop that signed nothing looked
exactly like a stop with nothing to sign. It is replayed to stderr as before
and carried into the record.

A FILE AND NOT A LEDGER EVENT, which is the one decision worth arguing. A
ledger event is the better home on every path except the ones that need it
most: a failed signature and a refused credential ARE the deployment being
unreachable, and a record that needs the component that just failed is absent
exactly when it is read for.

Success writes nothing -- commit_recorded already covers it, and a second line
here would be a second source of truth for one fact. A failed retirement writes
nothing either: the reaper expires that run as run_expired (IP 6.7), so it
already has a durable record. What had none was the work. Every failure path
still exits 0, and nothing on any of them touches the index.

Driven by OPS-085..088 in scripts/hooks/shim-selftest.sh, written first and
observed red against the shim as it stood: 17 new assertions, all 17 red and
red on merit -- there was no record of any kind to read -- while the 64
pre-existing cases stayed green in both directions. 81 passed, 0 failed after.
The shim was driven under sh, bash, zsh and dash, which wrote byte-identical
records and exited 0 in all four.

